C = [
("h1", "12. 1 Timothy 2:8–15"),
("p", "Claim: 1 Timothy 2:11–15 forbids a woman to teach or to usurp authority over the man, and it gives the reason in the next two verses: Adam was first formed, and the woman was deceived. The reasons are drawn from Genesis, and they are not local. The volume argues that the limit stands for the Body’s assembly, and it reports the verse on childbearing as the passage has it."),
("sub", "12.1 The setting"),
("p", "The passage belongs to a letter written so that Timothy “mayest know how thou oughtest to behave thyself in the house of God, which is the church of the living God, the pillar and ground of the truth” (1 Timothy 3:15). It follows an exhortation to prayer “for all men” (1 Timothy 2:1) and the statement that there is “one God, and one mediator between God and men, the man Christ Jesus” (1 Timothy 2:5). Paul then gives what he wills of men and of women. “I will therefore that men pray every where, lifting up holy hands, without wrath and doubting” (1 Timothy 2:8). “In like manner also, that women adorn themselves in modest apparel, with shamefacedness and sobriety” (1 Timothy 2:9), with “good works” (1 Timothy 2:10). The setting is the church, and Timothy is the one told."),
("sub", "12.2 The instruction"),
("p", "“Let the woman learn in silence with all subjection. But I suffer not a woman to teach, nor to usurp authority over the man, but to be in silence” (1 Timothy 2:11, 12). Three things are said. The woman is to learn. She is to learn “in silence with all subjection.” And Paul does not allow her “to teach, nor to usurp authority over the man.” N. T. Wright draws attention to the first of the three, and he is right to: “The key to the present passage, then, is to recognise that it is commanding that women, too, should be allowed to study and learn, and should not be restrained from doing so (verse 11).” The volume agrees that verse 11 enjoins learning, and notes how much it asks for a woman of the church. It does not follow that verse 12 is a disclaimer, as the next sections show."),
("p", "The word “teach” is not unlimited in Paul. He says that the aged women are to be “teachers of good things; That they may teach the young women to be sober, to love their husbands, to love their children” (Titus 2:3, 4). He remembers the “unfeigned faith” that dwelt in Timothy’s grandmother Lois and his mother Eunice (2 Timothy 1:5), and that “from a child thou hast known the holy scriptures” (2 Timothy 3:15). The teaching forbidden in 1 Timothy 2:12 is therefore not every teaching. John Piper makes the point in the way the volume does, that Paul “didn’t mean every kind of teaching in every situation,” and ties the verse to the authority it names. The pairing in the verse supports him: “to teach, nor to usurp authority over the man.” The volume reads the two as the work of the one who teaches the church with authority, which the passages on the overseer name as his work (1 Timothy 3:2; 5:17; Titus 1:9)."),
("sub", "12.3 The reasons"),
("p", "“For Adam was first formed, then Eve. And Adam was not deceived, but the woman being deceived was in the transgression” (1 Timothy 2:13, 14). The word “For” joins the reasons to the instruction. They are given as the reasons for verse 12, and they are not given as an illustration of it. Two are named. The first is the order of creation: Adam first, then Eve. The second is the transgression: the woman, being deceived, was in it, and Adam was not deceived. The volume reads both as Paul reads Genesis. They are about the man and woman of the beginning, and Paul uses them as the ground of an instruction to Ephesus."),
("p", "Thomas Constable reads the first as a statement of leadership, “from the Creation onward it was God’s intention that the male should provide leadership for the female,” and he adds that this “implies no essential superiority of the male over the female.” He limits the scope: “I do not think that this is what Paul meant. He cited God’s intention for male/female relationships specifically in a marriage context, not in general social situations,” and the church applies it in “the household of God.” The volume agrees with him on the limit. It argues that Paul’s instruction is about the assembly and does not extend to every relation of man and woman in the world. Piper says of the same verses, “his support was not to argue from culture but from creation.” The volume agrees with that statement of the ground, and puts it as its own argument: the two reasons Paul states are drawn from Genesis, and neither is drawn from the customs of Ephesus."),
("sub", "12.4 Another reading of the verse"),
("p", "Wright reads verse 12 differently: “I don’t mean to imply that I’m now setting up women as the new authority over men in the same way that previously men held authority over women.” He finds a reason in the temple of Artemis at Ephesus, where, he says, the priests were women. The volume sets the reading beside the verse and answers it. The sentence says, “I suffer not a woman to teach, nor to usurp authority over the man, but to be in silence.” The phrase “I suffer not” states what Paul does not allow. It does not state what he does not mean. The next verse begins “For,” and gives creation as the reason. A reading in which the verse says only what Paul does not intend would leave the reasons of verses 13 and 14 without a point to prove. The verse as written states a prohibition, and the reasons stand under it."),
("sub", "12.5 Verse 15"),
("p", "“Notwithstanding she shall be saved in childbearing, if they continue in faith and charity and holiness with sobriety” (1 Timothy 2:15). The verse has been read in many ways, and the volume does not choose among them as a matter of doctrine. It notes two things the verse itself does. It changes from “she” to “they,” and it names as the condition “faith and charity and holiness with sobriety.” It must also be read with what Paul says in the same chapter and the one before: “Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners” (1 Timothy 1:15), and “one mediator between God and men, the man Christ Jesus” (1 Timothy 2:5). The volume does not read the verse as making childbearing the ground of a woman’s salvation. The faith that is named in the condition is the same faith of which the same letter speaks throughout, and the volume leaves the manner of the saving unspecified, as the verse does."),
("ctbl", "1 Timothy 2:8–15 in its parts.", [
    ("Verses", "What is said", "The ground stated"),
    ("2:8", "Men pray every where, lifting up holy hands", "None stated beyond “I will”"),
    ("2:9, 10", "Women adorn themselves in modest apparel and good works", "“which becometh women professing godliness”"),
    ("2:11", "The woman learns in silence with all subjection", "None stated here"),
    ("2:12", "No teaching, no usurping authority over the man; to be in silence", "Given in 2:13, 14"),
    ("2:13", "Adam was first formed, then Eve", "Creation"),
    ("2:14", "Adam was not deceived; the woman, being deceived, was in the transgression", "The transgression"),
    ("2:15", "She shall be saved in childbearing, if they continue in faith and charity and holiness with sobriety", "A promise with a condition; the manner is not stated"),
  ], [0.14, 0.50, 0.36]),
("sub", "12.6 What the volume takes from the chapter"),
("bul", [
    "Verse 11 enjoins that the woman learn, and verse 12 does not permit her to teach or to usurp authority over the man.",
    "The teaching forbidden is the teaching joined with authority, which the passages on the overseer name as his work.",
    "The two reasons, creation and the transgression, are drawn from Genesis and are not drawn from local custom.",
    "The volume argues that the limit stands for the Body’s assembly and extends no further than the assembly.",
  ]),
]
